# Hidden interview questions for Part 6 (Lessons 22–28). (lesson, level, question, trap, answer)
Q = [
 (22, 1, "What's the difference between WHERE and HAVING?",
  "Saying they're interchangeable.",
  "WHERE filters individual rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after aggregation, so it can use aggregates such as <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 5</code>. Filtering rows with WHERE first is also faster, because fewer rows are grouped."),
 (22, 2, "When would you use a LEFT JOIN instead of an INNER JOIN when building features?",
  "Defaulting to INNER JOIN.",
  "When every entity must stay in the training set even if it has no related events: customers with no purchases, houses with no viewings. An INNER JOIN silently drops them, which biases the data and loses a useful \"zero activity\" signal. After a LEFT JOIN, fill counts with 0 (COALESCE) but think about whether other missing values really mean zero."),
 (22, 2, "After joining customers to orders, total revenue looks too high. What happened?",
  "Blaming the data source.",
  "Probably a one-to-many (or many-to-many) join duplicated rows: each customer row is repeated once per order, and summing a customer-level column (or joining a second many-table) multiplies values. Aggregate the many side to one row per key before joining, and check row counts and key uniqueness before and after every join."),
 (22, 3, "How do you make sure features built from event logs don't leak?",
  "\"Use a random train/test split.\"",
  "Give every training row a cut-off time (when the prediction would have been made) and compute each feature only from events strictly before it, by putting the time condition in the join. Split train and test by time as well. Watch for fields that are updated after the outcome (status columns, \"last modified\" values) and for aggregates computed over the whole table."),
 (22, 2, "Write a query for each customer's most recent order.",
  "Using GROUP BY with MAX(date) and then selecting other columns that aren't grouped.",
  "Use a window function: <code>SELECT * FROM (SELECT o.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date DESC) AS rn FROM orders o) t WHERE rn = 1</code>. Ties need a tie-breaker in ORDER BY. A GROUP BY with MAX gives the date but not reliably the other columns of that row."),
 (23, 1, "What's the difference between a parameter and a hyperparameter?",
  "Using the words interchangeably.",
  "Parameters are learned from data during training (weights, split points). Hyperparameters are set before training and control the learning (λ, k, depth, learning rate, number of trees). Hyperparameters are chosen by validation, not by the training loss, since the training loss would always prefer the most flexible setting."),
 (23, 2, "Why does random search often beat grid search?",
  "\"Randomness explores better\" with no mechanism.",
  "Usually only a few hyperparameters really matter. A grid with $m$ values per dimension tests only $m$ distinct values of the important ones however large it gets, whereas $n$ random trials test $n$ distinct values of each. Random search also scales to many hyperparameters without the multiplicative blow-up, and any number of trials can be run."),
 (23, 3, "Your tuned model scored 0.95 in grid search but 0.90 on the test set. Why might that be?",
  "Only saying \"overfitting\".",
  "The best search score is optimistically biased: it's the maximum over many noisy estimates on the same folds. The test set is also a finite sample with its own noise. Other causes: leakage inside the search (preprocessing fitted outside the pipeline), or a test set from a different period or distribution. Use nested CV for an honest estimate and check the score's confidence interval."),
 (23, 3, "How does Bayesian optimisation decide what to try next?",
  "Saying it's \"like random search but smarter\".",
  "It fits a surrogate model of the validation score as a function of the hyperparameters (a Gaussian process, a tree ensemble or TPE's densities) and maximises an acquisition function, such as expected improvement, that rewards both a high predicted score and high uncertainty. Each new result updates the surrogate. It pays off when each trial is expensive."),
 (24, 2, "What's the difference between permutation importance and a tree's built-in feature importance?",
  "Treating them as the same number.",
  "Built-in (impurity) importance sums how much each feature reduced impurity during training: it's computed on training data and favours features with many distinct values, so noise can rank highly. Permutation importance measures the drop in a held-out score when the feature is shuffled: model-agnostic and honest, though correlated features can hide each other. Prefer permutation importance (or SHAP) for reporting."),
 (24, 3, "Explain Shapley values and the properties that make them attractive.",
  "Calling SHAP \"just feature importance\".",
  "A feature's Shapley value is its average marginal contribution to the prediction over all orders in which features could be revealed, with absent features filled in from a background. They satisfy efficiency (contributions sum to prediction minus baseline), symmetry, dummy (no effect, zero credit) and additivity, and are the unique attribution with these properties. Exact computation is exponential, so KernelSHAP samples and TreeSHAP exploits tree structure."),
 (24, 3, "A PDP shows a strong effect of feature A, but A is highly correlated with B. What could go wrong?",
  "Trusting the curve as-is.",
  "A PDP sets A to every value for every row while keeping B as it is, creating unrealistic combinations (high A with low B) that the model never saw, so the curve may reflect extrapolation. Credit may also be split arbitrarily between A and B. Use ALE plots or conditional methods, check ICE curves, and interpret A and B together."),
 (24, 2, "Your model's top feature is the patient's hospital ID. What do you do?",
  "Celebrating the high accuracy.",
  "Treat it as a red flag for a shortcut or leakage: the model may be learning which hospital treats sicker patients, or a data-collection artefact, which won't generalise to new hospitals. Remove or group the feature, validate on hospitals the model never saw (group split), and look for other proxies. Explanations are most valuable exactly for finding this."),
 (25, 1, "Why is accuracy a bad metric for fraud detection?",
  "Saying it's \"too high\".",
  "With 0.1% fraud, predicting \"not fraud\" for everything is 99.9% accurate and catches nothing. Accuracy is dominated by the majority class. Use recall, precision, average precision (PR-AUC) against its baseline, or a cost-based metric, at the threshold you will actually use."),
 (25, 2, "Class weights, oversampling or threshold moving: how do you choose for an imbalanced problem?",
  "Always reaching for SMOTE.",
  "Start with a well-calibrated model and pick the threshold from costs or an alert budget: this keeps probabilities meaningful. Try class weights or resampling when they improve average precision or cost on validation data, which happens more for flexible models. Always resample inside training folds only, and recalibrate if you need real probabilities afterwards."),
 (25, 3, "How does Isolation Forest work, and why does it suit anomaly detection?",
  "Describing it as a random forest classifier.",
  "It builds many random trees: each split picks a random feature and a random threshold between its min and max. Anomalies, being few and different, are separated from the rest in fewer splits, so their average path length is short; the score normalises that length. It needs no labels or distance computations, scales linearly, and handles many features."),
 (25, 3, "You deploy an anomaly detector and only review flagged cases. What problem does this create?",
  "Assuming recall can be measured from reviewed cases.",
  "You never learn about anomalies the system missed, so recall is invisible and any retraining on reviewed cases only reinforces what the detector already finds (a feedback loop). Review a random sample of unflagged cases, track known incidents from other sources, and keep some exploration in what you flag."),
 (26, 1, "Why can't you use ordinary k-fold cross-validation for a forecasting model?",
  "\"You can, if you shuffle.\"",
  "Shuffled folds train on days after the ones they test, and neighbouring days are similar, so the model effectively sees the future and the score is far too optimistic. Use time-ordered splits: train on the past, test on the next block, and repeat for several cut-off dates (backtesting)."),
 (26, 2, "What baseline would you compare a daily sales forecast with?",
  "Comparing only with a constant mean.",
  "The seasonal naive forecast (same day last week), plus the naive forecast and a moving average. Seasonal naive is often hard to beat. Report MASE, the model's MAE divided by the seasonal naive MAE, so below 1 means the model adds value."),
 (26, 3, "Your gradient boosting forecast is fine on old data but always too low recently, as sales grow. Why?",
  "Blaming not enough trees.",
  "Trees predict averages of training targets, so they can't extrapolate a rising level beyond what they've seen. Model the change or ratio from a lag (for example y / y_{t−7}), detrend first, or combine with a linear model for the trend."),
 (26, 2, "Recursive or direct multi-step forecasting: what's the trade-off?",
  "Not knowing the difference.",
  "Recursive uses one one-step model and feeds its predictions back as lags: simple, but errors can compound. Direct trains one model per horizon on features available that far ahead: no compounding, but more models and less use of recent data. Report accuracy by horizon either way."),
 (27, 1, "What's the difference between collaborative filtering and content-based recommendation?",
  "Calling them the same thing.",
  "Collaborative filtering uses only interaction patterns (users who liked this also liked that), so it needs history. Content-based uses item features (text, category, price) to find similar items, so it works for new items. Production systems usually combine both."),
 (27, 2, "Why is RMSE on ratings a poor way to evaluate a recommender?",
  "Saying RMSE is fine because it's standard.",
  "Users see a short ranked list, so what matters is whether the top few items are relevant: precision@k, recall@k, NDCG or hit rate on held-out interactions, split by time. RMSE weighs all items equally, including ones never shown, and ratings are missing not at random."),
 (27, 3, "Explain matrix factorisation for recommendations.",
  "Describing it as PCA on a filled-in matrix.",
  "Each user and item gets a vector of k latent factors; the predicted rating is the global mean plus user and item biases plus the dot product of the vectors. It's fitted only on observed entries, with L2 regularisation, by SGD or alternating least squares. The factors capture tastes such as genre without being told."),
 (27, 3, "How would you handle cold start for a new item?",
  "\"Wait until it has ratings.\"",
  "Use its content features (text, category, price) to place it near similar items or to predict its latent factors, show it to a small exploration share of users, and update quickly as interactions arrive. For new users, use popularity, context and a few onboarding questions."),
 (28, 1, "What's the difference between a credible interval and a confidence interval?",
  "Saying they're the same.",
  "A 95% credible interval contains the parameter with 95% probability given the data and the prior. A 95% confidence interval comes from a procedure that covers the true value in 95% of repeated samples; this particular interval either contains it or doesn't."),
 (28, 2, "How would you run a Bayesian A/B test on conversion rates?",
  "Only computing a p-value.",
  "Give each version's rate a Beta prior, update it to Beta(a + conversions, b + non-conversions), and draw samples from both posteriors to estimate P(B > A), the expected lift and the expected loss of choosing B. Decide when the expected loss falls below a threshold, and check that reasonable priors give the same decision."),
 (28, 3, "Why is ridge regression a Bayesian method in disguise?",
  "Saying it isn't related.",
  "With Gaussian noise of variance σ² and a Gaussian prior N(0, τ²) on the weights, the posterior mean (and MAP) of the weights is the ridge solution with λ = σ²/τ². A Laplace prior gives lasso at the MAP. The full Bayesian treatment also gives the posterior covariance, and so uncertainty for each prediction."),
 (28, 3, "What's Thompson sampling, and when would you use it instead of an A/B test?",
  "Calling it random allocation.",
  "For each decision, sample a plausible value of each option's reward from its posterior and choose the best sample; update the chosen option's posterior with the result. It shifts traffic to better options while still exploring uncertain ones. Use it when you want to earn while learning (headlines, offers); use an A/B test when you need an unbiased, precise estimate of each option."),
]
